{"entries":[{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-1","title":"John 5:1","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":1,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":1},"content":"<p>1 Jesus on the sabbath day cures him that was diseased eight and thirty years. 10 The Jews therefore cavil, and persecute him for it. 17 He answers for himself, and reproves them, shewing by the testimony of his Father, 31 of John, 36 of his works, 39 and of the scriptures, who he is.</p>\n<p>2:13; Ex 23:14-17; 34:23; Le 23:2-4; De 16:16; Mt 3:15; Ga 4:4</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-2","title":"John 5:2","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":2,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":2},"content":"<p>market. or, gate. Ne 3:1; 12:39 pool. Isa 22:9,11 Bethesda.The supposed remains of the pool of Bethesda are situated on the east of Jerusalem, contiguous on one side to St. Stephen's gate, and on the other to the area of the temple. Maundrell states that, \"it is 120 paces long, and forty broad, and at least eight deep, but void of water. At its west end it discovers some old arches, now damned up. These some will have to be porches, in which sat that multitude of lame, halt, and blind. But it is not likely, for instead of five, there are but three.\"</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-3","title":"John 5:3","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":3,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":3},"content":"<p>of blind. Mt 15:30; Lu 7:22 withered. 1Ki 13:4; Zec 11:17; Mr 3:1-4 waiting. Pr 8:34; La 3:26; Ro 8:25; Jas 5:7</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-4","title":"John 5:4","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":4,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":4},"content":"<p>whosoever.The sanative property of this pool has been supposed by some to have been communicated by the blood of the sacrifices, and others have referred it to the mineral properties of the waters. But, 1. The beasts for sacrifice were not washed here, but in a laver in the temple. 2. No natural property could cure all manner of diseases. 3. The cure only extended to the first who entered. 4. It took place only at one particular time. 5. As the healing was effected by immersion, it must have been instantaneous; and it was never failing in it effects. All which, not being observed in medicinal waters, determine the cures to have been miraculous, as expressly stated in the text.</p>\n<p>first. Ps 119:60; Pr 6:4; 8:17; Ec 9:10; Ho 13:13; Mt 6:33; 11:12 Lu 13:24-28; 16:16 was made. 2Ki 5:10-14; Eze 47:8; Zec 13:1; 14:8; 1Co 6:11; 1Jo 1:7</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-5","title":"John 5:5","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":5,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":5},"content":"<p>thirty. 14; 9:1,21; Mr 9:21; Lu 8:43; 13:16; Ac 3:2; 4:22; 9:33; 14:8</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-6","title":"John 5:6","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":6,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":6},"content":"<p>and knew. 21:17; Ps 142:3; Heb 4:13,15 Wilt. Isa 65:1; Jer 13:27; Lu 18:41</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-7","title":"John 5:7","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":7,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":7},"content":"<p>I have. De 32:36; Ps 72:12; 142:4; Ro 5:6; 2Co 1:8-10 before. 4; 1Co 9:24</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-8","title":"John 5:8","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":8,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":8},"content":"<p>Mt 9:6; Mr 2:11; Lu 5:24; Ac 9:34</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-9","title":"John 5:9","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":9,"chapterEnd":5,"verseEnd":9},"content":"<p>immediately. 14; Mr 1:31,42; 5:29,41,42; 10:52; Ac 3:7,8 and on. 10-12; 7:23; 9:14; Mt 12:10-13; Mr 3:2-4; Lu 13:10-16</p>"},{"id":"tsk-jhn-5-10","title":"John 5:10","author":"R. A. Torrey (editor)","reference":{"book":"JHN","chapterStart":5,"verseStart":10